HST Office: its own Windows automation names; no LibreOffice automation name is shared.

HST Office registered LibreOffice's automation class objects and ProgIDs, so with both suites
installed the last installer served com.sun.star.ServiceManager, Writer.Application and
Calc.Application for both, and uninstalling one broke the other's automation clients.

New class ids, generated once for HST Office and never changed:
- HSTOffice.ServiceManager (and .1): {BAB2DE94-F543-4763-B27C-C88CA8B1E606}, also its AppID
  (was com.sun.star.ServiceManager, {82154420-0FBF-11d4-8313-005004526AB4});
- HSTOffice.Writer.Application: {46108D44-89EA-4AC1-8190-DF4362FBAFCC} (was Writer.Application,
  {82154421-...});
- HSTOffice.Sheets.Application: {EE151D41-E628-4D5C-BD96-9BD4B596C916} (was Calc.Application,
  {82154425-...}); its LocalServer32 key now belongs to the Sheets module, as its ProgID does.

The old names are not kept: external scripts use the new ProgIDs. The class objects are
registered at run time in extensions/source/ole/servprov.cxx; the sw/sc vbaapplication.cxx
coclass ids (IProvideClassInfo) and the ActiveX control's CoCreateInstance follow. The
AppID\soffice.exe key is dropped: it maps an executable name, which every suite with a
soffice.exe shares, and it carried no AppID value. The interface ids of the VBA objects stay.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).

